      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point \\( A \\) and point \\( B \\) in a rectangular prism with dimensions 10 inches by 20 inches by 10 inches, we can use the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. The formula for the diagonal \\( d \\) of a rectangular prism with length \\( l \\), width \\( w \\), and height \\( h \\) is:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n\\]\n\nHere, the dimensions of the rectangular prism are:\n- Length \\( l = 20 \\) inches\n- Width \\( w = 10 \\) inches\n- Height \\( h = 10 \\) inches\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the squares of each dimension:\n\n\\[\n20^2 = 400\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nNext, we sum these squares:\n\n\\[\n400 + 100 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of the sum to find the length of the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\), we find the prime factorization of 600:\n\n\\[\n600 = 2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the square root by taking out pairs of prime factors:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5^2} = \\sqrt{2^2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2} = \\sqrt{2^2} \\times \\sqrt{5^2} \\times \\sqrt{2 \\times 3} = 2 \\times 5 \\times \\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the diagonal is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
