      "text": "The diagonal of the rectangular prism can be found using the 3D version of the Pythagorean theorem. \n\nFor the side with dimensions 10 inches by 10 inches, the diagonal d1 is given by:\n\\[ d1 = \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{100 + 100} = \\sqrt{200} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nFor the side with dimensions 20 inches by 10 inches, the diagonal d2 is given by:\n\\[ d2 = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{400 + 100} = \\sqrt{500} = 10\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\nThe total length of the diagonal connecting points A and B is the square root of the sum of the squares of d1 and d2:\n\\[ \\text{Total diagonal} = \\sqrt{(10\\sqrt{2})^2 + (10\\sqrt{5})^2} = \\sqrt{200 + 500} = \\sqrt{700} = \\sqrt{100 \\cdot 7} = 10\\sqrt{7} \\]\n\nSo, the length of the diagonal is \\(\\boxed{10\\sqrt{7}}\\).\n\nLet's verify the steps and calculation with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Dimensions of the base rectangular prism\nlength_base = 10\nwidth_base = 20\n\n# Calculate the diagonal of the base\ndiagonal_base = math.sqrt(length_base**2 + width_base**2)\n\n# Dimensions of the height of the rectangular prism\nheight = 10\n\n# Calculate the diagonal of the rectangular prism\ntotal_diagonal = math.sqrt(diagonal_base**2 + height**2)\nprint(total_diagonal)\n```",
