      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) in the rectangular prism, we first need to determine the lengths of the prism's sides. The dimensions of the rectangular prism are given as 10 inches by 20 inches by 10 inches. The diagonal \\(d\\) of a rectangular prism can be found using the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem, which states that the diagonal is the square root of the sum of the squares of the three dimensions. Mathematically, this is expressed as:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(l\\), \\(w\\), and \\(h\\) are the lengths of the sides of the rectangular prism. Substituting the given dimensions into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares of the dimensions, we have:\n\n\\[\n10^2 = 100, \\quad 20^2 = 400, \\quad 10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together gives:\n\n\\[\n100 + 400 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nNow, we take the square root of 600:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{100} \\times \\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting point \\(A\\) and point \\(B\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
